// The sandbox shape of a signed <all-permissions/> Java Web Start application:
// the application's own code base is fully trusted, and everything else in the
// JVM gets only what the default policy grants — which is nothing that matters.
//
// That asymmetry is the point. An agent injected into such a JVM lands on the
// SYSTEM class path, so its protection domain is an ordinary file code source
// with no grant here, and it is denied environment reads, property reads and
// socket accept exactly as a JNLPSecurityManager denies them. Measured against
// OpenWebStart 1.14, the first denial is the same one:
//
//     Denying permission: ("java.lang.RuntimePermission"
//                          "getenv.PLATYNUI_AGENT_DIR")
//
// This is used with -Djava.security.policy=<this file> (single '='), so it
// EXTENDS the JDK's own java.policy rather than replacing it — again matching a
// real target, which has its base policy too.
//
// `platynui.fixture.classes` is passed by whoever launches the fixture, because
// a checkout lives wherever it lives. It must be a URL-shaped path: forward
// slashes on every platform, no trailing slash. A codeBase that matches nothing
// loads WITHOUT ANY ERROR and silently leaves the fixture sandboxed as well,
// which is a harsher and different shape than the one under test — so the
// fixture asserts its own permissions at startup (`--require-security-manager`)
// instead of trusting this file to have applied.
grant codeBase "file:${platynui.fixture.classes}/-" {
    permission java.security.AllPermission;
};
